@@ -126,6 +126,15 @@ REBROWSER_PATCHES_WEBDRIVER_FIX=0
 ```
 *Note: this only affects browsers launched by the library. If you connect to an existing browser, pass the switch yourself.*
 
+### Match `navigator.userAgentData.brands` to a custom user agent
+Playwright only. When you set `userAgent` on a context, Playwright sends Chrome the user agent string and platform hints but no brand list, so Chrome keeps its own: `navigator.userAgentData.brands` and the `Sec-CH-UA` header name the browser you actually launched (for Playwright's bundled Chromium, only `Chromium`) at its real version, whatever version your user agent claims. A page that compares the two sees the mismatch.
+
+For a `Chrome/<major>` user agent the patch sends the brand list that Chrome of that major version sends, built the way Chrome builds it (`GenerateBrandVersionList` in Chromium's `user_agent_utils.cc`): `Google Chrome`, `Chromium` and the GREASE brand, in the order and with the GREASE name and version that major version uses. For example, `Chrome/131.0.0.0` gives `"Google Chrome";v="131", "Chromium";v="131", "Not_A Brand";v="24"`. It covers pages and service workers. Without a `userAgent` override nothing changes. Edge (`Edg/`) and Opera (`OPR/`) user agents are left alone.
+```shell
+# use 0 to disable this patch and keep the library's default behavior
+REBROWSER_PATCHES_UA_BRANDS_FIX=0
+```
+
 ### Method to access browser CDP connection
 Sometimes, it could be very useful to access a CDP session at a browser level. For example, when you want to implement some custom CDP command. There is a method `page._client()` that returns CDP session for the current page instance, but there is no such method for browser instance. 
 This patch adds a new method `_connection()` to Browser class, so you can use it like this:
